    <!-- APP VIDEO AD TIPS -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:8px">App Video Ad <span class="cap y">Tips</span></h2>
      <p style="color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);margin-bottom:24px">Actionable advice from producing thousands of app install ads across every major platform.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;gap:20px">
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:16px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin-bottom:8px">Optimal Ad Length by Platform</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">TikTok: 15-21 seconds is the sweet spot — long enough to show the app, short enough to maintain completion rates above 25%. Meta (Reels and Feed): 20-30 seconds gives you room for a proper hook, demo, and CTA without losing mid-funnel viewers. YouTube Shorts: 30-45 seconds because the audience expects slightly more depth. For YouTube in-stream pre-roll, front-load everything into the first 5 seconds before the skip button appears, then reward viewers who stay with a full walkthrough. Never make a 60-second ad unless you have retention data proving your audience watches that long.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:16px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin-bottom:8px">Screen Recording Best Practices</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">Always record on a real device, not an emulator or simulator — users can tell the difference. Use a clean demo account with realistic-looking data (not "Test User" or empty states). Record at 60fps for smooth scrolling and tap animations. Zoom into the part of the screen that matters — a full-phone screenshot at 9:16 makes text unreadable on smaller devices. Add subtle zoom animations (1.2-1.5x) to draw attention to specific features. Remove the status bar or use a clean one (full battery, strong signal, no notifications) to avoid visual clutter. Record multiple takes of each interaction and use the smoothest one.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:16px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin-bottom:8px">Showing App UI Without Overwhelming</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">The biggest mistake in app ads is showing too much UI at once. A full dashboard screenshot means nothing to someone who has never used your app. Instead, show one feature at a time with a clear text overlay explaining what it does. Use progressive disclosure: start with the simplest screen (onboarding or home), then drill into one feature that solves the core problem. Blur or dim surrounding UI elements to focus attention on the active area. If your app has a complex interface, consider a motion graphics version that rebuilds the key screens in a simplified, visually clean format rather than screen-recording the actual dense UI.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:16px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin-bottom:8px">UGC vs Motion Graphics: When to Use Each</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">UGC (creator-led content) works best when trust and relatability matter — consumer apps, lifestyle products, anything where "a real person uses and likes this" is the key message. Motion graphics work best when explanation matters — complex workflows, data visualization, multi-step processes, or any app where the value is hard to see in a quick screen recording. The highest-performing app ad campaigns use both: UGC for top-of-funnel awareness and trust-building, motion graphics for mid-funnel education and feature highlighting. If you can only afford one format, start with UGC for consumer apps and motion graphics for B2B/productivity apps.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:16px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin-bottom:8px">App Store Screenshots vs Video Ads</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">Your video ads and your app store page need to tell the same story. If someone watches an ad about your AI scheduling feature and lands on an app store page highlighting your note-taking feature, you lose the install. Align the primary value proposition across both touchpoints. However, do not repurpose app store preview videos as paid ads — they are designed for a different context (someone already considering your app) and perform poorly in a feed where you need to earn attention. App store videos should be polished product demos. Paid ads should feel native to the platform they run on. Build them separately but keep the messaging consistent.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:24px;background:var(--off-white,#f7f7f7);border-radius:12px;border:1px solid var(--border,#e6e6e6)">
          <strong style="display:block;font-size:16px;color:var(--dark,#111);margin-bottom:8px">Sound-On vs Sound-Off Optimization</strong>
          <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--soft,#4d4d49);line-height:1.6;margin:0">On Meta, 80% of feed video views happen with sound off. On TikTok, it is the opposite — most users have sound on. Design every ad to work in both modes. For sound-off: use bold text overlays (minimum 48px on 1080p), burned-in captions for any spoken content, and visual storytelling that communicates the value without narration. For sound-on: use trending audio on TikTok, clear voiceover on Meta, and ensure music does not compete with speech. The best app ads read as a silent infographic and play as an engaging video — they work both ways without compromise.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:48px 24px">
      <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);margin-bottom:24px">Frequently Asked <span class="cap p">Questions</span></h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How much do video ads for apps cost?</summary>
          <div class="ans">
            <p>Our app video ad packages start at $500 for 10 videos. This includes scripting, screen recording or creator production, editing, captions, and delivery in platform-native formats (9:16 and 4:5). For larger-scale creative testing programs — the kind where you need 30-50 ads per month with weekly sprints and performance analysis — pricing starts at $2,000/month. The exact cost depends on the mix of formats (screen recordings are less expensive than creator shoots, which are less expensive than motion graphics), the number of variations per concept, and whether you need us to handle creative strategy and competitive research or just production.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do you handle screen recordings for our app?</summary>
          <div class="ans">
            <p>We set up a dedicated demo account in your app with realistic sample data — no empty states, no "Test User" placeholders. We record on real iOS and Android devices at 60fps using professional screen capture tools. Before recording, we plan the exact user flow with your team: which screens to show, which features to highlight, what interactions to demonstrate. We record multiple takes of each flow and select the smoothest one. If your app requires login credentials, backend data setup, or special configurations, we work with your team to prepare everything before the recording session. All screen recordings are reviewed for visual quality, pacing, and accuracy before they go into the edit.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What platforms do you produce ads for?</summary>
          <div class="ans">
            <p>We produce video ads optimized for Meta (Facebook and Instagram — Feed, Stories, and Reels), TikTok (In-Feed and TopView), YouTube (Shorts and in-stream pre-roll), Snapchat, and Apple Search Ads. Each platform gets creative designed specifically for its format, pacing, and audience behavior. We deliver in all required aspect ratios: 9:16 for vertical placements, 4:5 for Meta feed, 1:1 for Instagram feed, and 16:9 for YouTube pre-roll. We do not simply crop or letterbox a single video — each aspect ratio gets a purpose-built composition so text, UI elements, and focal points are always positioned correctly.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is there a minimum order?</summary>
          <div class="ans">
            <p>The minimum is 10 videos for $500. However, for app creative testing to be effective, we recommend starting with at least 20-30 videos across 3-4 concepts and multiple formats. This gives the algorithm enough creative diversity to find winning combinations. A single batch of 10 videos might produce 2-3 winners, but you need at least 3-4 testing sprints to build a reliable creative framework for your app. Most of our app clients settle into a rhythm of 20-40 new videos per month after the first two months of testing.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do you produce App Store and Google Play preview videos too?</summary>
          <div class="ans">
            <p>Yes, we produce ASO (App Store Optimization) preview videos alongside paid ad creative. App store preview videos have different requirements than paid ads — they need to be product-focused, show real app functionality without promotional overlays, and follow Apple and Google's specific technical guidelines (30 seconds max for iOS, specific resolution requirements). We design ASO videos to complement your paid ads: the same value proposition, consistent visual language, but formatted for the app store context where the viewer is already considering your app. We recommend updating your ASO videos every time you find a new winning ad angle, so the store page reinforces whatever message drove the user there.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

